WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.930
There's so much to look at and think about
in Disney's long-awaited sequel that it's

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.570
easy to overlook the many jokes, references,
and shoutouts hidden throughout.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:14.090
Fortunately, we're here to help you make sense
of it all with this look at some of the best

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:17.920
and most memorable Easter eggs in Frozen II.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.810
It's almost as obvious that Frozen II is a
Disney movie as it is obvious that it's a

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:27.630
movie about a queen with magical powers who
turns stuff into ice and belts out "Let it

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.089
Go" to make herself feel better.

00:00:29.089 --> 00:00:34.130
Yes, the House of Mouse signed the paychecks
for all the animators and voice actors who

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:39.790
worked very hard to make Frozen II a dazzling
cinematic experience, and the crew said thanks

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:44.900
by dropping in a number of cute references
to Disney projects old and new.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.520
The film begins with a pre-Frozen flashback
to Anna and Elsa as kids.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.840
Although they live in a magical fairy tale
kingdom of yore, they do what kids of any

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.480
time would do: play with dolls.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.880
But these dolls are made out of ice, courtesy
of Elsa's magical powers.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.570
One of the figures is a little elephant with
a long trunk and oversized ears — Dumbo,

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:06.920
in other words.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.700
A slightly hulking, rounded one is a dead
ringer for Baymax, the friendly robot from

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.210
the 2015 Disney/Marvel hit Big Hero 6.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:21.180
There's also one who looks like adorable monster
Totoro from My Neighbor Totoro, a 1988 Studio

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.740
Ghibli film to which Disney controlled American
distribution rights.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:26.820
"Merchandising!"

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.060
"Merchandising, what's that?"

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.140
"Merchandising!"

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.090
Of course, the most recognizable character
in Disney's long history is Mickey Mouse.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:41.829
Walt Disney's first successful creation is
so recognizable and so popular that the silhouette

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:47.530
of this high-voiced star of "Steamboat Willie"
is a linchpin of Disney's iconography.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:52.049
Disney theme parks are full of "Hidden Mickeys"
— those three black circles that comprise

00:01:52.049 --> 00:01:57.600
the mouse's head and big ears are real-life
Easter eggs to be found on the ground, walls,

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.319
and inside rides.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:03.090
Just like Disneyland, Frozen II is sprinkled
with a few Mickeys.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:08.530
During a game of charades, Olaf cycles through
several poses, one of which involves placing

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.939
coals on top of his head and one in his nose
region.

00:02:11.939 --> 00:02:16.280
The clue he's acting out is "mouse," and he
chose to look like Mickey.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:21.540
Later, when Elsa sings "Into the Unknown,"
she spins and leaves an ice circle in her

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.540
wake.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:28.510
Very briefly, two circles appear atop that
circle, forming the well-known Mickey shape.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:33.060
Tons of Disney animated movies, including
Sleeping Beauty, Snow White and the Seven

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.250
Dwarfs, Mulan, and Cinderella, are based on
folk tales, legends, and fairy tales.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:44.000
Frozen is based on The Snow Queen, a story
first published in Danish in 1844.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.959
The author, Hans Christian Andersen, also
wrote the source material for another Disney

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:50.900
classic, The Little Mermaid.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:56.640
He laid the narrative groundwork for Frozen,
but Frozen II was created entirely by modern-day

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.590
screenwriters imagining new adventures for
the characters inspired by Andersen's.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:07.240
The author's presence is still felt in Frozen
II, however, as an affectionate passing reference.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:12.040
Toward the end of the movie, audiences see
a flashback to when Elsa and Anna's parents

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.709
— Iduna and Agnarr — were children.

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:19.890
Iduna runs over to Agnarr and inquires about
the book in which he's happily engrossed.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.659
He explains it's "a new Danish author."

00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:25.410
He means Hans Christen Anderson.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:31.209
Pixar is a wholly-owned subsidiary of Disney,
so Disney distributes Pixar films such as

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:33.489
Toy Story, Cars, and Newt.

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:35.230
Oh, you haven't heard of Newt?

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.549
That's because it's a long-lost, unfinished
project.

00:03:38.549 --> 00:03:43.840
Announced in 2008, Newt was about the world's
last male and female blue-footed newts that

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.159
must breed to save their species, only they
don't get along.

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:54.499
Pixar had no choice but to cancel the film
— Fox's 2011 release Rio hit theaters first,

00:03:54.499 --> 00:03:58.920
with virtually the same plot as Newt, except
it was about macaws.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.030
Frozen II just may have paid homage to this
forgotten obscurity.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:07.470
One of the cutest new characters introduced
in the film is a big-eyed magical salamander

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:11.939
named Bruni who can spread fire the way Elsa
spreads ice.

00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:16.180
He bears more than a strong resemblance to
the newts introduced in the concept art of

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.310
the ill-fated Newt.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.120
The original Frozen was a massive hit.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:26.260
Upon its release in 2013, it earned $1.2 billion
at the box office.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:31.180
And it's full of memorable songs — "Do You
Want to Build a Snowman" and the Oscar-winning

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:36.500
"Let It Go" especially — so adapting Frozen
into a Broadway musical, like The Lion King

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.090
and Aladdin before it, was a no-brainer for
Disney.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:45.620
The live version of Frozen opened in 2018
and earned three major Tony Award nominations,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.590
including one for Best Musical.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:52.870
Co-starring in the challenging role of conflicted,
heartbroken young Anna in that production:

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:54.750
actress Mattea Conforti.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.250
When Frozen II producers put its cast together,
they brought Conforti on board.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.780
In a subtle Easter egg that only the most
hardcore of Frozen franchise fans were likely

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:09.800
to notice, the former young Anna gave voice
to young Elsa in Frozen II.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:15.240
Apart from Elsa and Anna's epic quest, Frozen
II also finds Kristoff wrestling with his

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.100
romantic situation.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.931
His relationship with Anna is seemingly so
solid that he wants to propose marriage, but

00:05:21.931 --> 00:05:25.900
every time he gets a chance to pop the question,
he messes it up.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:30.270
Before long, he wonders if he and his love
are drifting apart, and he expresses these

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:34.430
feelings in a big musical number called "Lost
in the Woods."

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:39.169
It's performed in the style of a 1980s power
ballad, and the sequence makes use of the

00:05:39.169 --> 00:05:42.880
kinds of things one would see in a vintage
'80s music video.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:45.360
Kristoff voice actor Jonathan Groff explained:

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.130
"It's sort of for the adults.

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.330
It's a joke for the adults.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.060
The little kids don't really get it."

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:51.301
"Were you living your best animated life doing
that?"

00:05:51.301 --> 00:05:52.560
"I was living my best animated life."

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.040
There's also one very specific reference to
a well-known video from the 1970s.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:02.840
The face of Kristoff, partially shadowed against
a black screen, appears in the bottom of the

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:07.199
screen, while three reindeer, singing backup,
show up in the top half.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:11.419
It's a visual straight out of Queen's "Bohemian
Rhapsody."

00:06:11.419 --> 00:06:17.580
Frozen II is surprisingly dark and emotionally
raw for a Disney cartoon aimed at grade-school-age

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:18.580
kids.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:23.629
It finds Elsa and Anna coming to terms with
their family's history and legacy, while Olaf

00:06:23.629 --> 00:06:29.129
the goofy snowman seems to be going through
an existential crisis brought about by aging

00:06:29.129 --> 00:06:32.330
and being able to live in environments other
than snow.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:36.800
When Elsa heads out into the Enchanted Forest
to find the source of a creepy voice only

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.470
she can hear, Anna, Kristoff and Olaf come
along, too.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:46.720
For a few moments, Frozen II turns into a
road movie, with the gang piling into a reindeer-drawn

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:47.720
cart.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:52.699
Olaf tries to help pass the time and entertain
his friends by reciting a seemingly endless

00:06:52.699 --> 00:06:56.110
litany of fun facts of varying levels of truth.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:00.500
He's having so much fun he doesn't care if
anybody wants to hear them or not.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:05.940
This whole sequence is a pretty faithful re-creation
of the famous scene from the 1996 dramedy

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:10.970
Jerry Maguire, in which Jerry and Dorothy
patiently listen to Dorothy's son recite trivia

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:11.970
bits like,

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:15.600
"Jerry, Did you know the human head weighs
eight pounds?"

